{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## /rebar-debug RCA — PROVEN mechanism; classified NOT-AN-AUTO-FIX (contract decision) → ESCALATED\n\n**Investigated on current `origin/main` @ `82a0d5cd328f` in an isolated worktree. Not a duplicate of\n6fe7** — `6fe7-956f-4901-45cf` (unreal-milky-sloth) fixed the **MCP** surface only and its own root\ncause names this CLI exposure as filed separately here. This ticket is the CLI-surface sibling.\n\n### Proven root cause (runtime-reproduced, not just read)\n`rebar show <id>` default `--output json` renders the ticket dict with\n`print(json.dumps(out, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False))` at\n`src/rebar/_engine_support/reads_cli.py:207`. `out` carries `created_at` / `updated_at`\n(and `comments[].timestamp`, `last_reopened_at`, `source_created_at` when present) as Python `int`\nnanosecond values from `time.time_ns()`. Python's `json.dumps` emits them as **bare 19-digit JSON\nintegers > 2**53-1**, which RFC 8259 §6 does not guarantee across implementations.\n\n### Discriminating proof (real float64 consumers, live)\nAgainst this very ticket on `origin/main`:\n```\nSTRUCTURAL unsafe ints (|n| > 2^53-1): 2\n  $.created_at = 1787860170488898642\n  $.updated_at = 1787948098205216001\nnode  -e require(...).created_at  -> 1787860170488898600   (ROUNDED, -42 ns, silent)\njq    '.created_at + 0'           -> 1787860170488898600   (ROUNDED under arithmetic)\npython float64                     -> 1787860170488898560   (ROUNDED)\n```\n`node`'s plain `JSON.parse` and `jq` under arithmetic both silently corrupt the value; only\n`int()`/`BigInt()`/jq-1.7-literal preserve it. Mechanism confirmed.\n\n### Legitimacy — NOT a straightforward defect; this is a public-contract decision\nThe current CLI behaviour is **working-as-documented**, so the not-a-bug guard does NOT unlock an\nautomatic fix:\n- **`docs/release-notes.md:22-24`** (BREAKING entry for the 6fe7 MCP fix, 2026-08-27) states\n  verbatim: *\"On the **MCP surface only**, any integer outside the JS-safe range is now emitted as\n  its exact decimal string. **CLI `--output json` and the Python library are UNCHANGED and still\n  emit integers.**\"* The integer emission here is the deliberately, explicitly scoped behaviour.\n- **`docs/api-stability.md:23`** designates `--output json` the **\"Strongest contract\"** and makes a\n  field **retype** an explicit **BREAKING** change requiring a release-notes + CHANGELOG entry.\n- **`src/rebar/schemas/ticket_state.schema.json`** types these fields `[\"integer\",\"string\",\"null\"]`,\n  so a bare integer is **schema-valid** today. (Its WIRE-FORM prose prescribes the string form when\n  out-of-range — that prose is the very policy this ticket exists to extend to the CLI.)\n\nThe exposure is a **real interoperability hazard** (silent float64 rounding on the surface where\nusers are explicitly invited to pipe rebar JSON into jq/node), but whether the CLI *should* switch\ninteger→decimal-string is a consumer-visible BREAKING **retype across CLI/library/MCP surfaces** —\nexactly the cross-surface contract/vocabulary decision the acceptance criteria call for\n(\"Decide and record the policy...\"). Per the /rebar-debug not-a-bug guard, that is handed to a\nhuman, not auto-landed. **No code changed; nothing pushed to Gerrit.**\n\n### Recommended disposition (for the human decision)\n**Option A (recommended): adopt the schema's WIRE-FORM string on CLI too.** Route\n`rebar show`/`list`/`ready`/`search`/etc. `--output json` through the existing, proven MCP seam\n`js_safe_result` (`src/rebar/_mcp_errors.py:152`, guard installed by `install_js_safe_guard`), so\nout-of-JS-range integers become exact decimal strings. This makes the single canonical schema's\nWIRE-FORM contract literally true on the \"strongest contract\" surface and closes the hazard.\nCompatibility mirrors the already-accepted MCP change: consumers coercing with `int()`/`BigInt()`\nare unaffected (`int(\"123\")==int(123)`); only naive `Number(x)`/raw-int consumers change — and they\nwere already reading corrupted values. Record it by extending the `docs/release-notes.md` entry from\n\"MCP surface only\" to \"CLI `--output json` too\", plus CHANGELOG, and add CLI regression coverage +\na structural guard so the surface cannot drift.\n- **Option B:** keep bare integers and prominently document the jq/`Number` hazard (docs-only).\n- **Option C:** add an opt-in flag (e.g. `--js-safe`) — most conservative, adds surface area.\n\nI recommend **A**: it removes a silent data-corruption footgun and unifies the three surfaces behind\none canonical schema, at a compatibility cost identical to the MCP change already shipped.\n\n### Attribution\nNot attributed (`caused_by` intentionally unset): the not-a-bug guard classifies this as an\nintended-behaviour/contract decision, not a `defect`/`incomplete-implementation`, so there is no\nescaped defect to attribute. For lineage only: the split-surface exposure was scoped this way by the\n6fe7 MCP fix (`unreal-milky-sloth`), which this ticket already records via `discovered_from`.\n\n### Sibling / parity census (Step 7) — no new work\nMCP = fixed (6fe7); **CLI = this ticket (escalated)**; Python library facade = N/A (returns Python\narbitrary-precision ints, no JSON boundary); reconciler/Jira outbound = probed clean in the ticket's\nNotes. No additional live sibling surfaces found.\n\n**STATUS: ESCALATED — awaiting a human contract/policy decision (Option A/B/C). Left `in_progress`\n(claimed) rather than closed, because the resolution is a design decision, not further agent work.**"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787948361656879001,"uuid":"b3a1972b-5f51-4c4d-9731-607863a32d5d"}